# NON-REGRESSION PIN — ⚠ GREEN BEFORE AND AFTER THE ALIAS-RESOLUTION FIX.
# This fixture is NOT red-verifiable against the pre-fix compiler, and it is
# labelled as such deliberately. What it pins is stated below; do not read it
# as coverage for the alias-spelling defect (the four
# `cow_alias_spelled_*` fixtures carry that).
#
# CELL: TEMPORAL ORDER — the alias is bound BEFORE the root is reassigned,
# and the view is taken AFTER.
#
#     Vector[String] alias = v      # alias aliases v's storage
#     v = []                        # reassign: severs, alias takes its own copy
#     String s = alias.get(0).unwrap()   # view taken AFTER the sever
#     ... grow the NEW v ...
#     print(s)                      # must still be the OLD element
#
# ⚠ "SEVERED" IN THE NAME DESCRIBES THE PROGRAM'S SHAPE AND THE USER-VISIBLE
# VALUE SEMANTICS -- NOT A COMPILER MECHANISM. No sever runs here. Measured:
# `cow_register_alias` (the sole producer of `BorrowOrigin::Alias`) is called
# ZERO times for this program, `cow_has_aliases` is false at the `v = []`
# reassignment, and `cow_sever_all_aliases_from` is never entered. Deleting
# the `v = []` line makes `cow_register_alias` fire, which is the positive
# control proving the instrument is live.
#
# WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENS: a bare-identifier bind takes the CoW ALIAS branch
# (`src/ir/lowering/stmts/mod.rs:1498-1509`) only when neither side is
# reassigned on a forward path (`is_cow_unsafe_at`). `v` IS reassigned below,
# so `alias` never becomes one -- it is eagerly cloned at the bind and owns
# its buffer from the start. The reassignment's PRESENCE PREVENTS the alias
# from forming; it does not sever one.
#
# WHAT IT PINS, AND WHAT IT CANNOT: the END-TO-END behaviour only -- the copy
# keeps the pre-reassignment value, the view reads it, and the new `v` goes
# its own way. That is worth pinning and it is all this program pins.
#
# ⚠ NO BREAK-THE-MECHANISM RED EXISTS FOR IT, and the reason is STRUCTURAL,
# not semantic: the provenance code a break would target IS NEVER REACHED for
# this program. Three separate breaks were built and run, all leaving this
# fixture rc 0 `hello|1|65`:
#   1. deleting `unset_ownership(builder, alias)` before the materialize in
#      `cow_sever_all_aliases_from`;
#   2. suppressing the `register_local` name rebind in `cow_materialize_alias`;
#   3. both at once.
# ⚠ Their inertness carries NO information about provenance soundness in
# either direction -- it is dead code for this input. Do not read it as
# evidence that the machinery is robust.
#
# Two earlier versions of this header got this wrong in turn: the first
# asserted (1) as both the mechanism and the way to see this go red; the
# second correctly retracted that but attributed the inertness to the buffer
# never being mutated again. Both were false, and a header that hands the
# next engineer a command which silently does nothing is worse than one that
# says nothing -- it reads as confirmation.
#
# ⊕ For a cell with a LIVE mechanism and a fire count, see
# `cow_view_into_owned_collection_survives_growth.gg` -- the owned copy itself
# reallocs after the view is taken, so the lazy rescue must be emitted.
#
# The two lengths are printed as well as the string: they pin that the sever
# gave `alias` its own 1-element copy while `v` went on to 65, i.e. that CoW
# value semantics held across the reassignment.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    Vector[String] v = []
    v.push(mk("hel", "lo"))
    Vector[String] alias = v
    v = []
    v.push(mk("new", "one"))
    String s = alias.get(0).unwrap()
    int i = 0
    while i < 64:
        v.push(mk("fill", "er"))
        i = i + 1
    print(s)
    print(alias.len())
    print(v.len())
